00:00:01.839 --> 00:00:03.429 align:start position:0%
 
you're watching the daily climate show  on sky news  on today's programme the uk's dirty  secret we report on the swamps of toxic  waste  the environment agency doesn't have a  record of  how climate change could be contributing  to the united states mega drought  and how cotton production is at risk  from global warming  hello and welcome to the uk's only daily  climate news show where we track the  changes  happening to our world right now and we  meet those developing  the solutions coming up the tuneful way  one company is persuading delivery  drivers to go green  but we start today with the story behind  the uk's acid  tar lagoons these toxic waste dumps are  environmental nightmares  but little is being done to clean them  up skies inzamam rasheed has the story  this is an acid tar lagoon a swamp  made up of 60 000 tons of toxic waste  from oil refineries near chester it's a  toxic  waste dump it's absolutely vile to be  around here there's nothing living in  there  anything that's close to being alive is  covered in  oil and tar for 60 years this pit of  poisonous gunge  has been blighting the lives of those  who live nearby  obviously i've got kids so the  environment for the health of the kids  it's obviously not great  being there it's literally a few hundred  yards down the road so  it's not great for anyone that lives in  this area at all according to the  environment agency  there are only two acid tar lagoons in  this  country for our investigation found  councils across the uk have officially  identified 34.  we took samples to see how dangerous  they really are  this is litmus which will indicate the  ph and as you can see  it goes uh to a bright red color  indicating a ph of one to two  so this is a highly acidic material for  a long time acetar lagoons have been a  hazard  to the natural environment controlled  waters  and in some instances human health they  now cost tens of millions of pounds to  clean up  and no one is taking responsibility  the environment agency told us the  identification of these sites  sits with local authorities however when  asked we will work collaboratively to  inspect the land to ensure risks to the  environment are understood  and the area remains safe acid lagoons  may have been around for more than half  a century  but little is known and being done about  our past actions  well if you want to see the full report  on the uk's acid  tar lagoons including the green solution  that's being used to help clean up one  site in  derbyshire then you can head over to our  youtube channel that's youtube.com  sky news well let's take a look at some  of the day's other climate news now  and moscow has been hit with a heat wave  temperatures reaching record-breaking  highs some parts of the city  saw temperatures hit 34.7 degrees  celsius  on monday that matches a record for a  day back in june  1901. scientists say that heat waves are  becoming more frequent  and more intense as global temperatures  rise  over time the un says the world's oceans  will continue to be destroyed if urgent  changes aren't made in the way that  they're managed  a report from the un environment  programme suggests 80 percent of marine  and coastal pollution  comes from land-based human activities  it's calling on governments to introduce  policies to reduce the effects that  these activities have  on coastal resources and to support the  transition  to a sustainable ocean-based economy  marine workers in taiwan have started  cleaning up an  oil spill off the country's southwest  coast  state-owned cpc corps said that the leak  occurred at its refinery in tallinn  on tuesday morning that's after bad  weather had caused a crack  in the pipeline it's hoped that the  prompt response by workers will minimize  the environmental impact caused by the  spill  more than half of teachers in england  want to encourage children to take  direct action against climate change  that's according to a new survey  research by the university of bristol  has found almost  all teachers believe in an  action-focused climate change curriculum  and 54 say that this should extend  to taking part in civil disobedience at  secondary school  and bird songs are being played on cargo  bikes across london  as part of a campaign to encourage  delivery drivers  to use low carbon transport 30 riders  have installed radios on their bikes to  play out the sounds of  gold finches blackbirds and bluetits  on all of their deliveries in july the  scheme aims to show people what cities  could sound like  if there were fewer cars and vans on the  road  to the united states now and president  biden has said that he'll bring  every resource to bear in dealing with  the severe drought and extreme heat  that's affecting the southwest  of the country the heat wave there has  broken hundreds of temperature records  increased the risk of wildfires and  affected water and electricity supplies  as well  and the country's biggest reservoir is  at record low  levels well we can show you here the  scale  of what is happening this is a map from  the u.s drought monitor from july  last year the lightest colors are no  drought  through to orange which shows you  moderate drought and the red color  shows extreme drought conditions but  this  is the picture this year in the west of  the united states you can see  vast swathes there the darkest red color  that shows exceptional drought as well  as far  more of the red extreme drought markings  well joining me now is professor  catherine hey ho chief scientist  at the nature conservancy in the united  states also  one of america's leading climate  scientists  professor hey ho good to talk to you  it's pretty drastic isn't it when you  see the difference of those  two maps there and this is before  the height of the summer  it absolutely is and of course we have  droughts and floods  naturally but what we are seeing is that  climate change is loading the weather  dice against  us making those droughts longer and  stronger than they would be otherwise  and also at the same time increasing our  heavy precipitation  and how do you explain scientifically  what is happening  as the world warms we know that it is  getting warmer and i know that sounds a  little bit obvious  but what that's doing is it's super  sizing our extreme heat waves here in  the united states extreme heat waves are  already three to five degrees fahrenheit  which is about um you know one and a  half to  almost three degrees celsius warmer than  they would be otherwise because of a  changing climate  so when a drought comes along that  drought and heat  reinforce each other building up a  stronger ridge that directs  us storm systems away from the region  leading to hotter and drier conditions  than we would ordinarily have it's been  long forewarned hasn't it by scientists  like you with your modelling have you  been surprised at how  quickly it's taken hold  it's no surprise what's happening but  the surprise is how quickly we are  seeing it happen with our own eyes in  the places where we live  today we can no longer say climate  change is a future issue  wherever we live here and now we are  seeing its effects  and they are affecting our lives it's  not only about the polar bears the ice  caps and sea level rise  it is literally about the food we eat  the air we breathe the water we drink  the safety of our homes we're looking at  incredibly high temperatures if this  kind of thing  continues do you see parts of the world  that effectively become  unlivable it would have to progress  considerably before we could see large  parts of the world that would become  unlivable  but that is possible if we don't heed  the warnings today  but these warnings are loud and clear  climate is changing and humans are  responsible we've known that for over a  hundred years  we also know now that the impacts are  here and now  but that there are solutions there truly  are solutions  that can wean us off our addiction to  fossil fuels that can help stabilize  climate  that can put carbon back into the soils  and the trees and the ecosystems where  we actually want the carbon  instead of up in the atmosphere where we  don't and that help us reduce our energy  use save money  and provide clean energy to the hundreds  of millions of people  who don't even have electricity today  there are  real solutions and those solutions are  good for us and that is an important  pairing with the risks professor  catherine hey ho  thank you very much thank you for having  me  now cotton makes up 31 of all the fiber  that's used in the textile industry  worldwide  and the sector directly supports the  livelihoods of  hundreds of millions of people but half  of all cotton growing regions  face severe climate risks by 2040 if  carbon emissions  continue to increase that's from the  first ever  global analysis of climate risks to the  industry  here's everything you need to know about  those findings in 60 seconds  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  and there's some positive news to bring  you today and that's that nearly  two-thirds of wind and solar projects  built last year  will generate cheaper renewable energy  than the most competitive  fossil fuel option the international  renewable energy agency  says that the cost of renewable  technologies is falling  every year with onshore wind power 13  cheaper in 2020 than it was in 2019.  in less than a decade the cost of  large-scale solar power  has fallen by more than 85  and according to the director general of  irena renewables are now  the cheapest source of power  well here on our data dashboard you can  see the real time  power mix for electricity in great  britain today  currently you can see that 47  is coming from fossil fuels 35  from renewables and 18 from  nuclear well that is everything from us  for today on the daily climate show  tomorrow i'll be speaking to the founder  of the restaurant chain oaxaca  thomasina myers about how changing the  way we farm  could help to save the planet that's at  the same time tomorrow  here on sky news see you then

